sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/approach/rule110-universality-machine-checked--grok-4-7--3> a prov:Plan, delphi:TestableHypothesis ;
    rdfs:label "Compose glider lemmas into the emulation theorem" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/rule110-universality-machine-checked> ;
    dct:description "Assuming the collision lemmas and the cyclic-tag theorem as already checked modules, formalize Cook's compiler: a function from a cyclic tag system and a finite input to a finite set of gliders on the background. The theorem is a lockstep bisimulation. Each tag step is a fixed finite sequence of the checked collisions, and the encoding of the work string is an invariant of that sequence. Prefer the Neary–Woods polynomial-overhead presentation where it cites the same gliders, and prove the polynomial bound as a separate lemma rather than hiding it in the invariant. This module is forbidden to re-verify collisions by simulation; it may only apply the lemmas from the catalog. That split is what makes a failure here different from a failure in the table." ;

    delphi:proposedTest "A no-sorry theorem: for every cyclic tag system and input there is a Rule 110 configuration whose evolution encodes the computation, with gliders, collisions and background used only as prior lemmas." ;
    delphi:context "Fails if: The invariant fails on some tag operation even though every individual collision lemma is true, which means the compiler, not the table, is wrong. Partial gates with no bisimulation do not count." ;
    delphi:hypothesisType "approach/proof" ;
    skos:note "Steps: 1. Define the compiler from cyclic tag systems to Rule 110 configurations using only named gliders. 2. State the representation invariant (work string, phase, and head glider). 3. Prove that each tag operation is a composition of catalogued collisions. 4. Prove the overhead bound, polynomial if the Neary–Woods schedule is the one formalized. 5. Compose with cyclic-tag universality to obtain the Rule 110 universality theorem and audit axioms." ;
    skos:note "Difficulty (proposer's estimate of completion within about a month of agent time): long-shot" ;
    skos:note "Missing: A month of agent time is unlikely to finish the bisimulation even after the collision lemmas exist. The missing piece is proof effort on the compiler invariant, not data or a new algorithm." ;
    skos:note "Tool: lean4" ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch11-14> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch11-27> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch01-07> ;

    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.25088/ComplexSystems.15.1.1> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1007/11786986_13> ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/ee98025a1960b300fcc06aa2106f51118b60f565/problems/approaches/grok-4-7.json> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/rule110-universality-machine-checked> a delphi:Concept, schema:Question ;
    rdfs:label "Machine-check the universality of Rule 110" .


  <https://doi.org/10.25088/ComplexSystems.15.1.1> dct:title "Universality in Elementary Cellular Automata" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Cook 2004, glider emulation of cyclic tag systems" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1007/11786986_13> dct:title "P-completeness of Cellular Automaton Rule 110" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Neary and Woods 2006, polynomial-time emulation and P-completeness of Rule 110" .
}

